after another night of icy temperatures  in kiev  protesters sang the ukrainian national  anthem to keep their spirits up  the government said it would not use  violence against the demonstrators  and last night it kept its promise many  protesters have reinforced their  barricades  and they're defiant  we want our nation to be self-sufficient  and free  the people should be in control of the  government not the other way round  pressure from abroad has mounted since a  police raid on tuesday  the u.s has been mediating and is also  considering sanctions  top eu diplomat katherine ashton has  held talks with the ukrainian president  in recent days  back in brussels she expressed optimism  that ukraine would after all  join an eu trade agreement  look janukovich made it clear to me that  he intends to sign the association  agreement  but ashton didn't provide details she  said the ukrainian deputy prime minister  would hold talks thursday or friday in  brussels  those challenges which are real russia  has maintained the pressure on ukraine  in his annual state of the nation  address however president vladimir putin  was less outspoken  i hope that all political forces in  ukraine can reach a deal in the interest  of its people  and solve all the current problems putin  added that russia wasn't forcing ukraine  to do anything it didn't want to do  moscow is unlikely to let go of ukraine  without a fight  since it's a crucial economic and  strategic partner  meanwhile the demonstrators who have  refused to give ground in kiev  made their decision long ago they want  ukraine to turn away from russia and  embrace the european union
 

